.TH YYLEX 3 "2026-04-02" "IEEE/The Open Group" "POSIX Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
yylex \- user-provided lexical analyzer for yacc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B int yylex(void);
.PP
.B extern YYSTYPE yylval;
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR yylex ()
function is a user-supplied routine required by parsers generated by
.BR yacc (1).
Its primary responsibility is to read characters from the input stream, assemble them into meaningful token units, and return the integer identifier of the current token to the parser.
.PP
Often,
.BR yylex ()
is automatically generated using the
.BR lex (1)
or
.BR flex (1)
utility, but it can also be handwritten in C.
.PP
When returning a token,
.BR yylex ()
must assign the corresponding numeric, textual, or structural value to the global external union
.BR yylval .
The parser uses the contents of
.B yylval
to evaluate the semantic actions associated with grammar productions.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The function must return a non-negative integer representing the unique symbol identifier for the parsed token.
The constants for these symbol identifiers are typically generated by
.B yacc
(using the
.B \-d
flag) in a header file such as
.IR y.tab.h .
Single character tokens can simply be returned as their ASCII integer bindings (e.g.,
.BR "return '+';" ).
.PP
When the end of the input stream is reached,
.BR yylex ()
must return
.B 0
to indicate the end-of-file condition to the parser, which instructs the parser to verify the completion of the start symbol production.
.SH SIDE EFFECTS
The primary side effect of
.BR yylex ()
is the modification of
.BR yylval .
It may also modify other global state mechanisms to track line numbers (e.g.,
.BR yylineno )
or column offsets for use in error reporting routines like
.BR yyerror ().
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <ctype.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include "y.tab.h"

int yylex(void) {
    int c;
    while ((c = getchar()) == ' ' || c == '\\t')
        continue;
    
    if (isdigit(c)) {
        yylval = c \- '0';
        while (isdigit(c = getchar()))
            yylval = yylval * 10 + (c \- '0');
        ungetc(c, stdin);
        return NUMBER;
    }
    
    if (c == EOF)
        return 0;
        
    return c;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yacc (1),
.BR yyparse (3),
.BR lex (1)
